WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.960
Thanks to Squarespace for sponsoring this video, where you can create your own website in minutes.

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:07.700
For many people, Apple is a confusing company:

00:00:07.709 --> 00:00:12.860
On one hand, it’s an unquestionable success
- creating and then dominating entire categories

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:16.539
of products, and making more money than any
other business in the world.

00:00:16.539 --> 00:00:17.539
By far.

00:00:17.539 --> 00:00:22.320
But somehow it also seems unable to understand
its customers: removing features we love,

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:26.849
neglecting its own products, and making bizarre
trade-offs we never asked for.

00:00:26.849 --> 00:00:30.980
So how can the world’s most profitable company
also be so foolish?

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:34.970
Many would say they can afford these mistakes
because of their exceptional marketing.

00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:40.000
Others see them as disorganized and distracted,
really only coasting on earlier success.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:44.450
These feelings are everywhere on the internet,
and frankly, they’re understandable.

00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:48.660
By being such a secretive company, they sacrifice
the chance to explain themselves, leaving

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:50.890
us to speculate from the outside.

00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:56.040
But theories like these only raise more questions:
If the goal is simply marketing to maximize

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:58.430
profit, why focus on so few products?

00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:02.300
They should be using their brand to sell anything
and everything, like Samsung.

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:04.390
And why remove things like the headphone jack?

00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:08.890
These are very deliberate decisions, yet they
guarantee far more negative press than they

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:10.830
make in sales of adapters.

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:15.210
Foolish as they sound, there must be some
kind of strategy here, it would take a miracle

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:19.270
for a truly dysfunctional company to be this
successful for this long.

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:23.160
Which makes sense: companies this big aren’t
driven so much by individual employees as

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:24.530
institutional principles.

00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:29.909
So, you may hate their strategy, think they’re
overpriced, and disagree with every decision

00:01:29.909 --> 00:01:33.700
they make, but by no means are they careless
or arbitrary.

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:37.880
The only way to make sense of all this - to
understand their success, predict their future,

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:41.340
and even explain their mistakes is to figure
out how they think.

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:44.869
And just three principles are enough to explain
almost everything.

00:01:44.869 --> 00:01:46.780
This is The Grand Theory of Apple.

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:53.760
Apple is treated as a technology company,

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:55.900
and anything else would seem ridiculous.

00:01:55.900 --> 00:02:00.310
But it would also be ridiculous to say its
goals are anything like other technology companies.

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:04.281
Their business may involve transistors and
resistors, but from their perspective, this

00:02:04.281 --> 00:02:06.100
is only incidental.

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:09.780
Technology is actually the enemy - a distraction,
a source of confusion.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:14.310
In the ideal world, no-one cares about RAM,
or even knows they’re using a computer:

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:16.980
they’re just drawing, or reading, or talking.

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:20.360
This may sound idealistic, but consider how
much it explains:

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:24.630
It’s why they’re obsessed with making
devices thin, light, and minimalistic.

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:26.810
Removing ports, bezels, and even buttons.

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:29.800
It also favors some devices over others.

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:34.420
Because from their perspective, each device
is fundamentally competing in the same category.

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:37.140
Which their executive Phil Schiller said quite
openly:

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:40.720
“The job of the watch is so you don’t
need to pick up your phone as often,

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:44.520
The job of the phone is to do more and more
things such that maybe you don’t need your

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:45.520
iPad,

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:49.340
The job of the iPad is to be so powerful and
capable that you never need a notebook,

00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:52.760
The job of the notebook is to make it so you
never need a desktop”

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:57.360
Each device has the goal of moving in this
direction of smaller, less confusing technology

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:00.270
by making it’s more bulky, confusing neighbor
obsolete.

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:04.300
They’ve been so slow to update desktops
because, well, they’re the furthest from

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:06.070
their vision of what a computer should be.

00:03:06.070 --> 00:03:10.330
There’s a huge group of fans and customers
who think Apple has changed, that they’re

00:03:10.330 --> 00:03:13.730
now obsessed with making devices thinner at
whatever cost.

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:18.570
But this was always the goal, it’s just
now becoming technically possible to do so.

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:23.160
And their products may overlap with competitors
now, but make no mistake: the second it’s

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:27.420
possible to remove what they see as an unnecessary
distraction, they will.

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:31.190
And as it becomes easier to do so, they’re
going to look less and less like a technology

00:03:31.190 --> 00:03:32.740
company at all.

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:39.260
When Steve Jobs came back to
the company in 1997, it was a mess: they were

00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:42.500
selling a million different products with
no clear strategy.

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:45.629
So he simplified the entire company into one
simple chart.

00:03:45.629 --> 00:03:49.300
They would make only one computer for each
market, but it would be really good.

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:53.050
They’ve grown much bigger since then and
they’ve had to expand that strategy.

00:03:53.050 --> 00:03:57.239
But compared to their competitors, they’re
still extremely focused, to the point of leaving

00:03:57.239 --> 00:03:58.459
money on the table.

00:03:58.459 --> 00:04:02.280
Their brand alone they could sell just about
anything, but they choose very selectively

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:04.150
where to focus their attention.

00:04:04.150 --> 00:04:08.670
Even a good idea is not enough to justify
their making it - they have to believe it’s

00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:09.670
huge.

00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:13.860
This constant fear of over-complicating things
is what allows them to put so much time and

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:16.070
so many resources into every project.

00:04:16.070 --> 00:04:20.320
They’re able to plan years and years ahead
this way, investing in features that won’t

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:22.770
see the light of day until far into the future.

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:27.100
And if they decide something no longer justifies
their time and attention, you wake up and

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:28.290
poof, it’s gone.

00:04:28.290 --> 00:04:29.800
Someday that will be the iPhone.

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:35.600
It’s
not that Apple doesn’t listen to feedback,

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:40.150
or even sometimes ask for it, but ultimately,
they consider their judgement more important

00:04:40.150 --> 00:04:41.150
than yours.

00:04:41.150 --> 00:04:45.720
This may seem incredibly stupid, but it’s
actually the biggest key to their success,

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:48.610
because without it, they could’ve only been
successful once.

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:53.150
Many companies create a very successful product,
but they milk it for as long as possible by

00:04:53.150 --> 00:04:55.130
giving whatever customers ask for.

00:04:55.130 --> 00:04:58.390
After Microsoft created Windows, they did
exactly this.

00:04:58.390 --> 00:05:01.860
They’re obviously doing just fine, but it
meant they weren’t ready when the smartphone

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:02.860
took off.

00:05:02.860 --> 00:05:06.660
Apple does just the opposite, they’re constantly
trying to compete with themselves.

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:11.440
Any other company would kill for the success
of the iPod, but Apple killed the iPod, with

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:12.440
the iPhone.

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:16.580
No one product lasts forever, so while it
hurts to kill your own success, it’s necessary

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:18.460
to stay on top in the long term.

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:22.690
This means not listening to your customers
asking for a better iPod, and instead creating

00:05:22.690 --> 00:05:24.590
what they don’t even know they want.

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:29.630
This can be a really tough pill to swallow,
especially in more tangible forms - the removal

00:05:29.630 --> 00:05:34.949
the floppy drive, CD drive, the old charger,
home button, headphone jack, and USB-A.

00:05:34.949 --> 00:05:39.949
People were angry, often for good reason:
these seem like needless sources of frustration.

00:05:39.949 --> 00:05:41.560
And you can bet Apple knows this.

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:44.610
But they also know they’re powerful enough
to survive these transitions.

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:48.400
They can afford to lose some customers and
receive some negative PR.

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:50.070
Because the up-side is HUGE.

00:05:50.070 --> 00:05:54.169
Journalists write that these changes are signs
of a problem, but the only true danger is

00:05:54.169 --> 00:05:55.169
if they stop.

00:05:55.169 --> 00:05:58.919
When they start listening to customers, they
lose the chance to make the next big thing.

00:05:58.919 --> 00:06:02.570
When the iPhone is released, they’ll still
be focused on improving the iPod.

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:06.610
Because, frankly, you and I aren’t going
to ask for the next big thing, we don’t

00:06:06.610 --> 00:06:07.699
know what it is.

00:06:07.699 --> 00:06:08.699
But Apple has to.

00:06:08.699 --> 00:06:12.639
To them, part of their job, the service they
offer, is saying “No, we’re not going

00:06:12.639 --> 00:06:14.449
to give you what you think you want”

00:06:14.449 --> 00:06:18.479
And again, this can be incredibly frustrating,
sometimes they’ve just wrong.

00:06:18.479 --> 00:06:22.210
The iPhone was late to the big phone market
because for the longest time, they thought

00:06:22.210 --> 00:06:23.860
they knew better than we did.

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:27.919
And this mentality can also create a bit of…
insensitivity.

00:06:27.919 --> 00:06:31.860
But it’s a risk they have to take, because
more often than not, ignoring the customer

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:33.000
has really paid-off.

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:39.820
A good theory not only makes
sense of the past but also makes testable

00:06:39.830 --> 00:06:41.590
predictions about the future.

00:06:41.590 --> 00:06:45.300
So if these principles really are at the heart
of Apple’s thinking, we can expect some

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:46.310
specific outcomes.

00:06:46.310 --> 00:06:51.169
Firstly, if Apple is motivated more by these
values than pure profits, we can expect them

00:06:51.169 --> 00:06:53.960
to keep making sacrifices that anger consumers.

00:06:53.960 --> 00:06:57.940
They’ll keep investing in the Mac until
a point where they think the alternative is

00:06:57.940 --> 00:07:02.690
better, whether that be a more powerful iPad,
a hybrid device, or something completely different.

00:07:02.690 --> 00:07:07.190
Before consumers are truly ready, they’ll
pull the plug on perfectly profitable devices

00:07:07.190 --> 00:07:11.000
to push towards the goal of smaller, less
complicated technology.

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:15.509
And right now as we speak, somewhere in California
Apple is working on something designed to

00:07:15.509 --> 00:07:17.590
kill their current bestsellers.

00:07:17.590 --> 00:07:21.139
Something designed to make their own business
obsolete.

